      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To present a rare case of the incidental discovery of two misplaced intrauterine devices in the same patient, and to discuss risk factors for uterine perforation and appropriate management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A 37-year-old woman complaining of chronic lower back pain presented with an X-ray of her abdomen showing two misplaced LNG-IUSs in the peritoneal cavity. More than two years before the patient had submitted within two months to two successive attempts at insertion of a Mirena® IUS. The threads of the first IUS were not seen at the follow-up visit and the IUS was not detected on ultrasound either. As it was assumed to have been expelled, insertion of a second IUS of the same type was attempted. Again, the threads were not seen at the control visit. This time, no further action was undertaken."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Radiography is helpful in confirming migration of an intrauterine contraceptive. Removal of the latter is recommended, due to the potential for serious complications."
        }
      ]},
